## 6. Function Structure Rule

Each function must include a purpose block.

Required structure:

```text
Purpose: Describe function.
Params : Describe parameters.
Return : Describe return value.
```

For PowerShell, this should usually be a comment-based block inside or immediately above the function.

For Python, this should usually be a docstring.

The function description must match the Stage 3 function contract.

---

## 7. Structured Data Comparison Rule

When comparing structured data, such as objects, arrays, lists, hashtables, dictionaries, or multi-field records, the preferred future design is to create a stable fingerprint/hash at assignment, capture, creation, or save time.

The fingerprint/hash should be stored together with the original data.

Future comparisons should prefer comparing the fingerprint/hash instead of repeatedly comparing the full object or array field by field.

The original object/array should be kept for debugging, display, evidence, and mismatch investigation.

The fingerprint/hash must be based only on fields or items that are part of the intended comparison identity.

Do not include volatile fields unless they are intentionally part of the identity.

Volatile fields usually include:

```text
Timestamp
LogTimestamp
RawTimestamp
PID
temporary index
runtime-only file name
session-only values
random IDs
```

Required priority:

```text
1. Preferred: fingerprint/hash created at assignment/capture/save time.
2. Acceptable: explicit field-by-field comparison when no fingerprint/hash was prepared.
3. Not acceptable for persistent/reloaded data: raw object reference comparison.
```

Retrofit rule:

Do not retrofit a new fingerprint/hash layer into an active repair or refactor unless the user explicitly approves it.

If the current implementation already uses explicit field-by-field comparison and there are open fixes in progress, finish the current repair first. Mark fingerprint/hash comparison as future cleanup instead of changing architecture mid-repair.

